D. Proof-facing cut lemma
  Atomize each E_safe component by even gates and odd branch-0 bad endpoints.
  Scalar overcount is legal only after the multiplicity sidecar is retained:
    uncovered atoms give branch0, multiplicity>=2 atoms give overlap tax.
  In this bank, every negative naive-slack row has a small odd-blocker
  rescue core whose own overlap tax beats the scalar deficit.  This is the
  Menger-cut target: prove that a primitive covering row either has
  nonnegative naive slack or has a bounded endpoint-labelled overlap core.
  HYP-3436 should supply the complementary two-color bad-core cover ledger;
  HYP-3437 supplies the one-branch overlap-tax lower-bound ledger.

E. Creative route interpretation
  Schwarz-Christoffel prevertices are the interval endpoints: useful as an
  arrangement/uniformization model, not as a continuous shortcut.
  Bring-radical language marks branch-monodromy debt: if a row needs a
  high-rank rescue core, do not solve it by scalar radicals; retain cuts.
  BDH/Mertens averaging can enter only as a mean-square theorem for the
  residue classes of these cut cores after endpoint exceptions are removed.
  The AP six-touch equioscillation becomes a boundary prevertex model for
  equality; the covering floor here is moved by the 2-adic branch atoms.

